Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Gothic period live-action; candlelight, deep shadows, desaturated palette

FADE IN:

INT. OLD HOUSE - HALL - NIGHT

A narrow Victorian hall. Candle smoke hangs in still air. NARRATOR, a gaunt man of thirty with hollow cheeks, dark disordered hair, and a black frock coat, stands rigid by a closed door, lantern in hand, eyes too bright.

(SOUND: distant floorboard creak, slow clock tick)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

He presses an ear to the wood. A vein jumps at his temple.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Moonlight leaks through fastened shutters. OLD MAN, frail and white-haired in a nightshirt, sleeps on a high bed. One eye shows beneath the lid: pale blue, filmed, vulture-like.

(SOUND: soft snore, wind against shutters)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad?

INT. OLD HOUSE - HALL - NIGHT

Narrator turns from the door, lantern closed, face lit from below. He speaks toward us, calm, precise, almost smiling.

(SOUND: lantern tin soft click)

NARRATOR
Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - DAY

Morning light. Old Man sits up in bed. Narrator enters with a tray, posture open, voice hearty—the same dark coat, hair combed now, smile fixed.

(SOUND: curtain rings, china rattle)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night.

Old Man's filmed blue eye catches the light. Narrator's smile freezes. His hand tightens on the tray.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me.

INT. OLD HOUSE - HALL - NIGHT

Narrator alone again, staring at nothing, fingers working.

(SOUND: low tense drone under the clock)

NARRATOR
I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

He mimes the eye with two fingers, then drops his hand as if burned.

NARRATOR
Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Midnight. The door opens a finger's width. Narrator's dark lantern, fully closed, slides into the gap, then his head—slow, inch by inch.

(SOUND: latch whisper, hinges almost silent)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me.

A thin ray cracks from the lantern and seeks the pillow. The Old Man's eye is closed. Narrator withdraws without a sound.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - DAY

Daybreak. Narrator throws the shutters wide, booming and kind. Old Man blinks the filmed eye against the light.

(SOUND: shutters bang open, street birds)

NARRATOR
And how have you passed the night, old man?

OLD MAN
Well enough. Well enough.

Narrator clasps his shoulder, laughing too loud. Old Man smiles, unsuspecting.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A second midnight. Again the head, again the ray—always on the closed eye. Narrator's face in the crack: triumphant, patient.

(SOUND: deathwatch beetle tick in the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

The ray holds. The eye stays shut. He eases back.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The eighth night. Pitch black—shutters tight. The door moves slower than a watch hand. Narrator's breath fogs the gap. He chuckles under his breath.

(SOUND: bedclothes sudden rustle)

Old Man jerks on the mattress, startled.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity.

Narrator does not draw back. He pushes on, steadily, steadily. His head is in. Thumb on the tin fastening—

(SOUND: sharp tin slip)

OLD MAN
Who's there?

Narrator freezes in the doorway, lantern unopened, not a muscle moving.

(SOUND: dreadful house silence, only his held breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down.

Old Man sits upright in total dark, listening. Narrator still as stone in the opening.

(SOUND: low stifled groan)

The groan wells from the bed—mortal terror, not pain.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. I knew the sound well.

Narrator's mouth twitches—pity and a private chuckle.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Still the standoff. Old Man upright. Narrator patient in the dark.

(SOUND: wind in the chimney, then nothing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had been saying to himself—“It is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor.”

A beat. Old Man does not lie down.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
All in vain; because Death, in approaching him had stalked with his black shadow before him, and enveloped the victim.

Narrator's fingers find the lantern. He opens a crevice—stealthily—until a single dim ray, thin as spider-thread, shoots out and falls full upon the vulture eye.

(SOUND: tiny hinge creak)

The eye is open—wide, wide open. Dull blue. Hideous veil. Nothing else of the face is lit.

(MUSIC: low pulse beginning, like a muffled watch)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it.

Narrator's free hand shakes. He steadies the ray on the damned spot alone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And have I not told you that what you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense?

(SOUND: low dull quick beat—watch-through-cotton)

The heartbeat fills the room. Narrator's eyes widen. He scarcely breathes. The lantern stays motionless.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now, I say, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too.

(SOUND: heartbeat louder, quicker)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the beating of the old man’s heart. It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage.

Sweat tracks Narrator's cheek. Still he refrains. The tattoo grows—louder, louder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Meantime the hellish tattoo of the heart increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant.

(SOUND: heartbeat pounding; house silence cracking around it)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now at the dead hour of the night, amid the dreadful silence of that old house, so strange a noise as this excited me to uncontrollable terror.

His jaw locks. A new fear hits—neighbors. The old man's hour has come.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I thought the heart must burst. And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour!

With a loud yell he throws the lantern wide and leaps in. Light blasts the bed.

(SOUND: yell; Old Man's single shriek; bedframe crash)

Old Man shrieks once—once only. Narrator drags him to the floor and heaves the heavy bed over him.

Narrator stands above the wreckage, smiling gaily.

(SOUND: muffled heartbeat under the bed, many minutes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In an instant I dragged him to the floor, and pulled the heavy bed over him. I then smiled gaily, to find the deed so far done.

He waits, head cocked. The muffled beat goes on—then falters—then ceases.

Narrator hauls the bed aside. He kneels, places a hand on the Old Man's heart, holds it there.

(SOUND: total silence where the beat was)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead. I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes. There was no pulsation.

He stares into the filmed eye, then covers it with the sheet.

NARRATOR
He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Later. A tub stands ready. Narrator works in silence by lantern light—sleeves rolled, movements precise, face intent. We see the saw, the covered tub, not the cuts.

(SOUND: wet cloth, careful saw strokes, no speech)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

He lifts three floor planks. Deposits the wrapped remains between the scantlings. Replaces the boards flush, runs a palm over the seams—no gap, no stain.

(SOUND: plank settle, soft laugh)

NARRATOR
There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

INT. OLD HOUSE - STREET DOOR - NIGHT

Four o'clock. Still dark. A bell sounds the hour. Then—knocking at the street door.

(SOUND: distant bell; firm triple knock)

Narrator descends with a light heart, lamp raised, coat restored.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
As the bell sounded the hour, there came a knocking at the street door. I went down to open it with a light heart,—for what had I now to fear?

He opens. Three men in period police coats: OFFICER REYNOLDS (bearded, senior), OFFICER HALE (younger, sharp-eyed), OFFICER BRIGGS (broad, quiet). They remove their hats with perfect suavity.

(SOUND: night air, distant cart)

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A shriek was heard by a neighbour during the night. Suspicion of foul play has been aroused. We are deputed to search the premises.

Narrator smiles—wide, easy—and bows them in.

NARRATOR
Gentlemen, welcome. The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Search—search well.

INT. OLD HOUSE - HALL - NIGHT

Narrator leads them room to room with a lamp, doors flung wide, gestures generous. Reynolds, Hale, and Briggs look, nod, find nothing.

(SOUND: boots on boards, doors opening)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I smiled,—for what had I to fear? I took my visitors all over the house. I bade them search—search well.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

He ushers them in last. Shows the undisturbed treasures—watch, coins, clothes press. Then, in wild audacity, he carries in chairs and sets his own seat precisely on the planks above the corpse.

(SOUND: chair legs scrape on the hidden boards)

NARRATOR
Rest from your fatigues, gentlemen. You see—all is secure.

The officers sit. Narrator sits on the fatal spot, singularly at ease, answering cheerily. They chat of familiar things—weather, the street.

(SOUND: polite low conversation, clock tick)

OFFICER HALE
Quiet night otherwise, then.

NARRATOR
Quiet as the grave, sir.

Narrator's smile holds. Then—a faint change. Color leaves his face. He touches his temple.

(SOUND: thin high ringing, under the chat)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears.

The officers still sit, still chat. The ringing grows distinct—more definite—until it is not in his ears alone.

(SOUND: low dull quick beat—watch in cotton—rising)

Narrator talks more freely, voice climbing. Pale now. The beat continues. Hale smiles politely. Reynolds nods at some trifle. Briggs watches Narrator's hands.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I found that the noise was not within my ears. No doubt I now grew very pale;—but I talked more fluently, and with a heightened voice.

He gasps once, covers it with a laugh. The sound increases. They do not seem to hear.

(SOUND: heartbeat louder under their calm voices)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Yet the sound increased—and what could I do? It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

He rises. Argues about trifles in a high key, violent gesticulations. Paces heavy strides. Foams. Raves. Swings his chair and grates it on the boards.

(SOUND: chair grate; heartbeat over all, louder—louder)

NARRATOR
Why—why do you linger over such nothing? The hour is late—surely—

The officers only chat on, pleasant, smiling. Narrator's eyes dart from face to face.

(MUSIC: pulse screaming under forced smiles)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It grew louder—louder—louder! And still the men chatted pleasantly, and smiled. Was it possible they heard not?

He stops over the spot. Hands claw the air. The beat is everything.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

(SOUND: heartbeat crushing; his ragged breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I could bear those hypocritical smiles no longer! I felt that I must scream or die! and now—again!—hark! louder! louder! louder! louder!

He shrieks, pointing at the floor beneath his feet.

NARRATOR
Villains! dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

The officers spring up. Narrator drops to his knees on the boards, clawing at the seams as the beat thunders on.

(SOUND: heartbeat peak; chair topple; sudden cut to ringing silence)

FADE OUT.

THE END
